BeatlesFan <smsumme@houston.rr.com> wrote:
> Another thing. Linux 
> only sees one of the four RZ29B hard drives.

"Only sees" that would mean that in /var/log/dmesg there is recorded
only one hardware device for your disks.  Is this really the case?

> The other three have 
> invalid partition tables according to Linux.

Well, maybe they are invalid.  What kind of partition tables are there,
if any?

> Any advice?

If you do not care about what is possibly on those disks then
repartition.  But I would rather start from getting CentOS.  A graphic
installer for that one should allow you to reparttion.

Remember that if you are booing via SRM then your boot disk has to have
"BSD disklabel" type of partitioning.  If you are using ARC/AlphaBIOS
then it needs to be "FAT table" type.  Nothing really to do with Linux
but with what your firmware can handle.  Non-boot disks can have any
type of partitioning which happens to be supported by your kernel (there
is quite a number of these).

  Michal


