"lpgl" <patrice.lespagnol@obs-nancay.fr> writes:

> Isn't accessing I/O ports, from user space, using outb/inb/outw/inw/
> after an initial  ioperm() or iopl(), an i386 only  feature ?
> I thought everything was memory mapped on alpha, and so were PCI
> devices.

The PCI I/O space is mapped to some high memory regions on Alpha.  The
outb/inb functions simply access these memory addresses.  These
functions are used for at least two reasons: 1) different machine
types map the I/O space at different addresses, and 2) for portability
to other architectures.

Another thing to be aware of on the Alpha is that the PCI memory is
mapped at a high offset as well.  You need to add a large number to
values listed by lspci to get the real address where a card can be
found, and use that when accessing /dev/mem.  Again, this offset
varies between machine types.  The function bus_base(), declared in
sys/io.h, will return the correct value.

It gets even more complicated.  Both PCI memory and I/O are mapped
to several CPU address regions.  Some of these are sparse, meaning
that a 32-bit write in that region will perform an 8-bit or 16-bit
write to the PCI bus.  The reason for this is that the first Alpha
models only had instructions to access memory 32 or 64 bits at a
time.

Then there's the IOMMU, but that's a different story, involving bus
mastering.

